# 1.1 What Is the Internet?

There are multiple ways to answer the question "What is the Internet?" One approach is to describe it in detail through its specific hardware and software components[cite: 1]. Another approach is to view the Internet as a networking infrastructure that provides services to distributed applications[cite: 1]. Let us begin our discussion with a hardware and software description using **Figure 1.1**[cite: 1].

## 1.1.1 A Nuts-and-Bolts Description

The Internet is a global computer network connecting billions of computing devices worldwide[cite: 2].

![Figure 1.1: A nut-and-bolts description of the Internet](computer%20networking%20part%201.pdf_page_2_image_1.png)
*Figure 1.1: A nuts-and-bolts description of the Internet.*[cite: 2]

In Internet terminology, all of these connected devices are referred to as **hosts** or **end systems**[cite: 2]. End systems are interconnected by a network of **communication links** and **packet switches**[cite: 2]. 

* **Transmission Rate:** Different links transmit data at different speeds, with the transmission rate measured in bits per second (bps)[cite: 2].
* **Packets:** When one end system needs to send data to another, the sending end system breaks the data into segments and adds header bytes to each segment[cite: 2]. These resulting data units are called **packets** in computer networking terminology[cite: 2]. The packets are then transmitted through the network to the destination end system, where they are reassembled into the original data[cite: 2].
* **Packet Switching:** A packet switch takes a packet arriving on one of its incoming communication links and forwards it onto one of its outgoing communication links[cite: 2]. 
  * The two most common types of packet switches on the Internet are **routers** and **link-layer switches**[cite: 2, 3]. Both types forward packets toward their ultimate destinations[cite: 3].
  * **Link-layer switches** are typically used in access networks[cite: 3].
  * **Routers** are typically used in the network core[cite: 3].
* **Network Path (Route):** The sequence of communication links and packet switches traversed by a packet from the sending end system to the receiving end system is known as a **network path** or **route**[cite: 3].

### Internet Service Providers (ISPs)
End systems access the Internet through **Internet Service Providers (ISPs)**[cite: 3]. Because the main purpose of the Internet is to interconnect end systems, the ISPs providing access to these end systems must also be interconnected with one another[cite: 3]. Upper-tier ISPs consist of high-speed routers interconnected by high-speed fiber-optic links[cite: 3].

